-- Catalog X-watch batch 2026-11-19 (Launchpad HQ)
-- Guarded SQL only. Platforms table. Idempotent. Update statements only.
-- No user tables. No inserts. No new slugs.
-- UPDATE: openai-api, chatgpt (Work and Codex note, then Accounting plugin),
--          gemini, github-copilot, tencent-hy4, suno, hugging-face, openrouter,
--          lm-studio, cohere, replit-agent
-- SKIP: Microsoft-Decision-1.1 (no catalog row for the model, Foundry, or Vercel).
--       An OpenRouter-stated end date for the Hy4 Flash discount (the model page
--       shows 50% off and does not state November 30; the clock time is Tencent Cloud's).
--       MAI Code 2 and Code 2 Flash cost comparisons (the post uses a promotional
--       price, publishes no per-token price, and gives no end date).
--       The words "private preview" for Cohere Video Intelligence (the post
--       recruits design partners and does not use those words).
-- Claude upgradeTriggers was fixed on 2026-11-16 and is not repeated.
-- Sources checked 2026-11-19 on vendor pages (see PR body). Not run from here.

UPDATE platforms SET description = CASE
  WHEN COALESCE(description, $xw$$xw$) = $xw$$xw$ THEN $xw$Note (2026-11-19): GPT-6.2 Astra (gpt-6.2-astra) and GPT-6.2 Sol (gpt-6.2-sol) were added to the OpenAI API on 2026-11-17. The model release notes say both accept text and images and return text, on the Responses API and Chat Completions, and the API changelog says tool calling requires the Responses API. Standard rates per 1M tokens, at or under 272K input tokens, are $8 input, $0.40 cached input, and $40 output for Astra, and $2 input, $0.10 cached input, and $10 output for Sol. The launch post says Astra's input and output prices are 20% lower than GPT-6 Astra, with a 60% discount on cached input, and that Sol keeps GPT-6.1 Sol's rates. The pricing page defines short context as 272K input tokens or fewer and long context as more than 272K input tokens. Above that line it lists $16 input, $0.80 cached input, and $60 output for Astra, and $4 input, $0.20 cached input, and $15 output for Sol, which is double the short-context input and cached-input rates and 1.5 times the short-context output rate. For these two models, Ultrafast costs 3 times the Standard rate through 2027-02-28, and 6 times the Standard rate starting 2027-03-01 (Pacific time). On the Responses API public beta, the 2-hour background completion window is 30% below Standard and the 8-hour window is 40% below Standard. The API changelog says that beta also covers GPT-6.1 Terra and GPT-6 Luna. The default soonest window is not part of the discount.$xw$
  ELSE description || $xw$ Note (2026-11-19): GPT-6.2 Astra (gpt-6.2-astra) and GPT-6.2 Sol (gpt-6.2-sol) were added to the OpenAI API on 2026-11-17. The model release notes say both accept text and images and return text, on the Responses API and Chat Completions, and the API changelog says tool calling requires the Responses API. Standard rates per 1M tokens, at or under 272K input tokens, are $8 input, $0.40 cached input, and $40 output for Astra, and $2 input, $0.10 cached input, and $10 output for Sol. The launch post says Astra's input and output prices are 20% lower than GPT-6 Astra, with a 60% discount on cached input, and that Sol keeps GPT-6.1 Sol's rates. The pricing page defines short context as 272K input tokens or fewer and long context as more than 272K input tokens. Above that line it lists $16 input, $0.80 cached input, and $60 output for Astra, and $4 input, $0.20 cached input, and $15 output for Sol, which is double the short-context input and cached-input rates and 1.5 times the short-context output rate. For these two models, Ultrafast costs 3 times the Standard rate through 2027-02-28, and 6 times the Standard rate starting 2027-03-01 (Pacific time). On the Responses API public beta, the 2-hour background completion window is 30% below Standard and the 8-hour window is 40% below Standard. The API changelog says that beta also covers GPT-6.1 Terra and GPT-6 Luna. The default soonest window is not part of the discount.$xw$
END, "updatedAt" = now() WHERE slug = $xw$openai-api$xw$ AND COALESCE(description, $xw$$xw$) NOT LIKE $xw$%GPT-6.2 Astra (gpt-6.2-astra)%$xw$;

UPDATE platforms SET description = CASE
  WHEN COALESCE(description, $xw$$xw$) = $xw$$xw$ THEN $xw$Note (2026-11-19): ChatGPT's release notes for 2026-11-17 say GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol are in preview in ChatGPT Work and Codex desktop. On Plus or Pro, either model can be chosen in the Work model picker or the Codex model selector. In a managed workspace, the models shown depend on the seat and workspace settings. Regular Chat keeps its existing model choices. The launch post says Plus, Pro, Enterprise, and Edu users get the models in ChatGPT Work and Codex. Pro 500 subscribers can use Ultrafast with either model. Ultrafast is also opening to Pro 200 subscribers, starting with GPT-6.2 Sol. Eligible Enterprise and Edu users get that Ultrafast access once a workspace admin enables it.$xw$
  ELSE description || $xw$ Note (2026-11-19): ChatGPT's release notes for 2026-11-17 say GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol are in preview in ChatGPT Work and Codex desktop. On Plus or Pro, either model can be chosen in the Work model picker or the Codex model selector. In a managed workspace, the models shown depend on the seat and workspace settings. Regular Chat keeps its existing model choices. The launch post says Plus, Pro, Enterprise, and Edu users get the models in ChatGPT Work and Codex. Pro 500 subscribers can use Ultrafast with either model. Ultrafast is also opening to Pro 200 subscribers, starting with GPT-6.2 Sol. Eligible Enterprise and Edu users get that Ultrafast access once a workspace admin enables it.$xw$
END, "updatedAt" = now() WHERE slug = $xw$chatgpt$xw$ AND COALESCE(description, $xw$$xw$) NOT LIKE $xw$%GPT-6.2 Astra and Sol are in preview in ChatGPT Work and Codex%$xw$;

UPDATE platforms SET description = CASE
  WHEN COALESCE(description, $xw$$xw$) = $xw$$xw$ THEN $xw$Note (2026-11-19): ChatGPT's release notes for 2026-11-18 say the Accounting plugin helps reconcile transactions, draft invoice entries, and prepare month-end reports from records and templates. Those notes say to open the ChatGPT desktop app on macOS or Windows, set up computer use, and install the plugin when the listing is available for the plan and workspace. In an Enterprise workspace, an admin must approve Accounting before members can install it. The help article says ChatGPT Business members can install it without prior admin approval, and that the plugin is free to install. The plugin page lists Intuit QuickBooks, QuickStart, NetSuite, Xero, BILL, FreshBooks, Rillet, and Campfire, and says four more apps are included without naming them.$xw$
